[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    181[0m:[38;5;10m[49m }
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    182[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    183[0m:[38;5;10m[49m // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    184[0m:[38;5;10m[49m // Phase B: the input classes the C program actually branches on.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    185[0m:[38;5;10m[49m //
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    186[0m:[38;5;10m[49m // `main` has a single branch point: whether `scanf("%d", &x)` converts a value
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    187[0m:[38;5;10m[49m // or leaves `x` at its initial 0 (matching failure or EOF). `driver` and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    188[0m:[38;5;10m[49m // `print_hex` are branch-free apart from the per-byte loop, whose only input is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    189[0m:[38;5;10m[49m // `sizeof(house_t)`. So the interesting classes are all scanf classes plus the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    190[0m:[38;5;10m[49m // integer conversion/truncation behaviour feeding `house.floors`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    191[0m:[38;5;10m[49m // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    192[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
