[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     16[0m:[38;5;10m[49m byte-for-byte the originals.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     17[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     18[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## What the program does
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     19[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     20[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `main` reads one integer with `scanf("%d", &x)` (starting from `x = 0`), passes
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     21[0m:[38;5;10m[49m it to `driver`, and always `return 0`. `driver` fills a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     22[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `{ int floors; int bedrooms; double bathrooms; }` with `{x, 3, 2.0}`, `memcpy`s
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     23[0m:[38;5;10m[49m it into a `char raw[sizeof(house)]`, and `print_hex` dumps those bytes as
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     24[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `%02x` each followed by one `"\n"`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     25[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     26[0m:[38;5;10m[49m On the x86-64 SysV ABI the struct is 16 bytes with fields at offsets 0, 4 and 8
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     27[0m:[38;5;10m[49m and **no padding**, so every dumped byte is determined by the input. The output
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     28[0m:[38;5;10m[49m is therefore always exactly 33 bytes: 32 lowercase hex digits plus a newline.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     29[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `stderr` is always empty and the exit status is always 0 — the program has no
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     30[0m:[38;5;10m[49m error path that changes either. That was confirmed empirically rather than
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     31[0m:[38;5;10m[49m assumed, including with stdin unreadable and stdout closed.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     32[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     33[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Mismatches found
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     34[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     35[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 1. Vertical tab (0x0b) was not treated as leading whitespace
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     36[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     37[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Status:** found and fixed.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     38[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     39[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `scanf`'s `%d` directive skips leading whitespace using C's `isspace`, which in
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     40[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the "C" locale is `' '` plus the control range `0x09..=0x0d`, i.e. `\t \n \v \f
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     41[0m:[38;5;10m[49m \r`. The Rust translation skipped whitespace with
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     42[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `(input[i] as char).is_ascii_whitespace()`, and Rust's `is_ascii_whitespace`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     43[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **deliberately excludes the vertical tab** (its documentation calls this out).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     44[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Consequence: for input `"\x0b42"` the C program skipped the `\v`, converted 42
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m and printed `floors = 42`; the Rust program saw a byte that was neither
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m whitespace, a sign nor a digit, treated it as a matching failure, left `x = 0`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m and printed `floors = 0`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m input: "\x0b42"
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m C   : 2a000000030000000000000000000040
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Rust: 00000000030000000000000000000040
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m        ^^^^^^^^ floors = 0 instead of 42
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Fix: added an explicit `is_c_space` helper implementing C's set
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m (`b == b' ' || (0x09..=0x0d).contains(&b)`) and used it for the skip, instead of
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Rust's `is_ascii_whitespace`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Regression coverage: `every_leading_byte_agrees` now runs all 256 possible
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m leading bytes through both programs, so any future divergence in the whitespace
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m classification is caught rather than depending on someone thinking of `\v`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m again. `whitespace_only_input` and `leading_whitespace_is_skipped` also include
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `\v` and `\f` explicitly.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Behaviours that look like bugs and were deliberately preserved
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m These were verified against the C binary and are reproduced, not "fixed".
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`scanf` failure is silently ignored.** `main` never checks the return value,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   so on EOF or a matching failure `x` keeps its initializer `0` and the program
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   prints `floors = 0` and exits 0. Empty input, whitespace-only input, `"abc"`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `"-"`, `".5"`, `"- 5"` and binary garbage all produce the `floors = 0` output
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   with exit status 0 — not an error.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Overflow saturates and is then truncated.** glibc converts `%d` into a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `long` (saturating at `LONG_MAX`/`LONG_MIN`, as `strtol` does) and stores the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   result through an `int *`, discarding the high 32 bits. So:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   | input | `long` value | stored `int` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   |---|---|---|
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   | `2147483648` | 2147483648 | `-2147483648` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   | `4294967296` | 4294967296 | `0` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   | `9223372036854775807` | `LONG_MAX` | `-1` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   | `99999999999999999999` | saturates to `LONG_MAX` | `-1` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   | `-99999999999999999999` | saturates to `LONG_MIN` | `0` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     88[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     89[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   Confirmed against the C binary for all of the above, plus digit runs up to
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     90[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   5000 characters long.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     91[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     92[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`scanf` reads across newlines.** Unlike `fgets`, `%d` skips any amount of
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     93[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   whitespace including newlines, so `"\n\n\n\n42\n"` yields 42. Covered by
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     94[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `scanf_reads_across_newlines`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     95[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     96[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Only the first token is consumed.** `"1 2"`, `"42abc"`, `"3.9"`, `"1e5"` and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     97[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `"0x10"` all convert just the leading decimal integer (`1`, `42`, `3`, `1`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     98[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `0`) and ignore the rest; `%d` is base 10, so `0x10` stops at the `0`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     99[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    100[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Arguments are ignored.** `int main()` takes no parameters, so `--help` and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    101[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   friends change nothing.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    102[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    103[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Test inventory
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    104[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    105[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `translation/tests/differential.rs` spawns **both** binaries as subprocesses,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    106[0m:[38;5;10m[49m feeds them the same stdin, and asserts stdout, stderr and exit status all match.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    107[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The Rust code is never loaded as a library. 28 tests, none `#[ignore]`d,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    108[0m:[38;5;10m[49m skipped or disabled.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    109[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    110[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Input classes covered: empty stdin; whitespace-only; a single value with and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    111[0m:[38;5;10m[49m without a trailing newline; `+`/`-` signs; every kind of leading whitespace
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    112[0m:[38;5;10m[49m including `\v` and `\f`; all 256 leading bytes; all 256 bytes as a digit-run
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    113[0m:[38;5;10m[49m terminator; all 256 bytes immediately after a sign; matching failures; trailing
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    114[0m:[38;5;10m[49m unconsumed text; leading zeros; the exact `int` boundaries; `long`-to-`int`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    115[0m:[38;5;10m[49m truncation; overflow saturation past `LONG_MAX`/`LONG_MIN`; digit runs of 18 to
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    116[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 5000 characters; embedded NULs and non-UTF-8 bytes; a 64 KiB input the program
